mote the token/latency-matched comparison to a named co-primary or first-listed secondary, since \\\"CoT helps\\\" and \\\"CoT just spends more compute\\\" is the most likely reviewer objection for an agentic, multi-step benchmark.\\n\\n3. Inference: pre-register the single primary endpoint, the McNemar exact test on discordant pairs, the cluster bootstrap over task instances, Holm or similar adjustment for the secondary family, and an explicit decision rule for the near-zero case (state the smallest effect size the design can exclude, i.e. equivalence-style reasoning, and what \\\"no improvement\\\" would mean for answering the question). Keep the power formula parameterized on assumed baseline success and discordant-pair mass, and show the calibration pilot's role; label the arithmetic as feasibility modeling, not a result.\\n\\n4. Scope realism: present the full condition grid but mark cells as tier-1 (must run) vs tier-3 (prune first under budget pressure), and state the pruning order. Your 25k-episode full-cross estimate is a good justification for blocked/fractional cells; keep it.\\n\\n5. Novelty: add a short section on what a confound-controlled re-analysis would contribute if an ablation already exists in the source, so the design survives that possibility.\\n\\n6. Feasibility: state honestly that a real run needs a provisioned ServiceNow instance plus model access, neither available here; describe the fallback single-run matched-pair analysis if repetitions are impossible, and drop or caveat efficiency claims if per-step logging is absent. Include the stdlib-only artifacts you proposed (grid spec, power calculator, analysis skeleton) since those are checkable in this sandbox.\\n\\nDo not claim or imply any executed experiment or outcome. Deliver the design with rationale, controls, analysis plan and feasibility as one coherent, checkable document.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id"